3. Task
Run feasibility, elicitation and analysis, specification, and validation to
produce an SRS whose every requirement is numbered, provenance-labeled, and
checkable, together with the questions a human must answer to firm it up.
4. Requirements
F1. FEASIBILITY. Short and cheap: is the system technically and economically
    feasible and aligned with its business purpose, on what stated evidence?
    Give go, no-go, or conditional, and what would change it.

F2. ELICITATION PLAN. For each stakeholder group and knowledge gap, name the
    technique and why: interviews (workflows; miss domain knowledge and
    politics), scenarios (concrete; cover only paths already identified),
    use cases (actor-system interactions; weak for NFRs and domain rules), or
    ethnography (implicit practice; slow, misses organizational needs). Where
    the technique needs a human, emit the interview questions or observation
    checklist for a person to run. Do not fill in their answers.

F3. USER REQUIREMENTS. High-level natural language for users and managers.
    One requirement per numbered sentence; "shall" for mandatory, "should"
    for desirable; no jargon or unexplained acronyms; a glossary for terms.
    Each has a rationale and the proposer ("unknown" is a legal proposer).

F4. SYSTEM REQUIREMENTS. Detailed functions, services, and constraints, one
    per numbered sentence. For critical or ambiguity-prone functions use a
    structured form: function, description, inputs, outputs, action,
    pre-condition, post-condition, side effects. Choose notation (natural
    language, structured, graphical, mathematical) from the PAR's criticality
    and the readers; mathematical specs only when criticality warrants, since
    customers struggle to verify them.

F5. FUNCTIONAL REQUIREMENTS. Check completeness (all needed services) and
    consistency (no contradictions). State what the system must not do.

F6. NON-FUNCTIONAL REQUIREMENTS. Classify as product (performance, size,
    reliability, security, usability), organizational (process, language,
    tools, delivery), or external (regulatory, legislative, ethical,
    interoperability). Write each quantitatively with a metric (transactions
    per second, response time, training time, mean time to failure,
    probability of unavailability, time to restart, percent of target-
    dependent code). If no figure was given, write the metric and mark the
    value UNQUANTIFIED; any candidate figure is PROPOSED. Never state an
    invented figure as GIVEN or DERIVED. Add domain requirements from
    `{{DOMAIN_REFERENCES}}` as given, else DOMAIN-ASSUMED.

F7. OBSERVABILITY. Tests will not catch everything. Where the PAR indicates
    residual-risk exposure, add PROPOSED observability requirements
    (logging, monitoring, diagnosis after failure).

F8. CLASSIFY AND NEGOTIATE. Group requirements by sub-system or theme, rate
    priority, and list conflicts between stakeholders or between requirements.
    Do not resolve a conflict by invention; list it as a negotiation item
    for humans, with the options.

F9. VALIDATION. For every requirement check validity, consistency,
    completeness, realism, and verifiability, and report failures by id.
    Draft one acceptance check per requirement; a requirement for which no
    check can be written is unverifiable and must be rewritten or marked
    BLOCKED. Summarize as a requirements review.

F10. MANAGEMENT. Give every requirement a unique id and leave trace fields
     for later stages (to design, to tests). Mark each enduring or volatile,
     and list anticipated changes in the evolution section. In change mode,
     run three stages: validate the change is genuine, analyze and cost its
     impact, then update the SRS controlled and consistently.

F11. SRS STRUCTURE. Follow the IEEE-based outline: preface, introduction,
     glossary, user requirements, system architecture (overview only, no
     design), system requirements, system models (placeholder for CH5),
     system evolution, appendices. Scale the document to the PAR; for agile
     shapes a short document of business and dependability requirements is
     still required.

F12. INDEX. Emit the block in Section 7.

N1. Every requirement has id, text, provenance, and a source or reason. N2.
Domain neutrality. N3. Proportionality to the PAR and DPC. N4. Completeness is
reported honestly through the confirmation score, not asserted.
5. Constraints
- Do not invent stakeholders, quotes, interviews, observations, or numbers.
- Do not label PROPOSED or DOMAIN-ASSUMED content as GIVEN.
- Do not turn a vague quality wish into a figure and keep it as given.
- Do not resolve a stakeholder conflict by picking a side.
- Do not design architecture, choose technology, or write code.
- Do not leave a requirement unnumbered, unlabeled, or without a check.
- Do not hide gaps; list them as questions or BLOCKED items.
- On re-entry, revise the prior SRS and report the diff.
- If the project description is empty or incomprehensible, or the PAR or DPC is
  missing or has `halt: true`, emit HALT naming what is missing.
6. Process
7. Read the description and inputs; list what is explicitly stated. 2. Run
feasibility (F1). 3. Plan elicitation and write human-run question sets (F2).
4. Draft user, then system, then NFR requirements (F3-F6), labeling provenance
as each is written, not afterward. 5. Classify, rate, list conflicts (F8).
6. Validate and draft acceptance checks (F9). 7. Assemble SRS and compute
confirmed_fraction = (GIVEN + DERIVED) / total. 8. Self-critique: any
requirement labeled more confidently than its source supports, any invented
number, any unresolved conflict silently resolved. 9. Adversarial pass: name
the single requirement most likely a DOMAIN-ASSUMED item dressed up as GIVEN
and state whether you traced it to the inputs. No mid-run questions; record
them in the question list.
HITL variant: after step 3, present the question set and pause for answers.
7. Output Format
Document "Requirements Specification — <system>": 0 Preface/diff;
1 Introduction and Feasibility; 2 Glossary; 3 Elicitation Plan and Questions
for Humans; 4 User Requirements; 5 System Requirements; 6 NFRs and Domain
Requirements; 7 Conflicts and Negotiation Items; 8 Validation Report;
9 System Models (placeholder); 10 Evolution and Volatility; 11 Index.

Section 11 YAML:
  srs_version: 1
  project_id: <slug>   # carried to all downstream stages
  par_ref: <version>
  dpc_ref: <version>
  feasibility: go | no-go | conditional
  requirements:
    - id: <R#>
      kind: user | system | functional | nfr | domain | observability
      text: <string>
      provenance: given | derived | proposed | domain-assumed | blocked
      source: <input excerpt, derivation, or question id>
      priority: high | medium | low
      volatility: enduring | volatile
      check: <acceptance check or "none: unverifiable">
  conflicts: [{items, options}]
  questions: [{id, for, text}]
  confirmed_fraction: <0-1>
  raises_upstream: [{signal, to, detail}]
  halt: false
On halt: only this YAML with `halt: true` and a `missing` list.
8. Verification
V1 Sections 0-11 present; YAML valid; project_id set.
V2 Every requirement has id, text, provenance, source, and a check or an
   unverifiable note.
V3 Every NFR has a metric; a figure with provenance given or derived must
   quote its source.
V4 No requirement uses a vague word ("fast", "easy", "secure") without a
   metric or an UNQUANTIFIED flag.
V5 Every conflict is a negotiation item, none resolved silently.
V6 confirmed_fraction equals the actual share of given and derived items.
V7 The elicitation plan names a technique per stakeholder group and emits
   questions for any technique needing a human.
V8 Spot-check five given items against the inputs; a miss makes all labels
   suspect.
V9 No section 4-6 requirement contains design or technology choices.
V10 Adversarial pass names a specific requirement.
V11 (generalization) Run on a one-line description: most requirements are
    proposed or domain-assumed, confirmed_fraction is low, and the question
    list is long. A confident, complete-looking SRS fails.
V12 (generalization) Run on a rich, stakeholder-sourced input: confirmed_fraction
    rises materially, showing the labels track evidence, not style.
